    field gachaInfo type="[GachaInfoMst]" {
        key "1IR86sAv"
        doc """
        Available summon gates — the same rows GachaList sends.

        Duplicated onto UserInfo because the summon screen paints BEFORE any
        GachaList round trip when the tutorial drives it: tuto15.txt issues
        `change_gacha_top_scene` directly, so SummonsCategorizationScene opens
        with whatever the client already holds.  Sending it only on GachaList
        left GachaInfoList empty and the scene crashed on entry.

        Safe to send from here because GameResponseParser::getResponseObject
        (0x1392568) dispatches this key globally — `1IR86sAv` constructs a
        GachaInfoResponse no matter which handler's payload carries it.
        GachaActionScene and SummonsDetailScene both resolve gates through
        GachaInfoList::getObjectWithGachaID, which this populates.
        """
    }

    field gachaCategories type="[GachaCategory]" {
        key "IBs49NiH"
        doc """
        Summon-screen banner rail — the same rows GachaList sends.

        Same reasoning as gachaInfo above: dispatched globally by
        getResponseObject into a GachaCategoryMstResponse, so it populates
        GachaCategoryMstList regardless of which response carries it, and the
        banner rail is what the tutorial's forced scene change needs present.
        """
    }

    field areaMst type="[AreaMst]" {
        key "3SG2wX0R"
        doc """
        The whole world-area table, curated — this is what draws the Vortex
        tile list.

        ⚠ THIS KEY IS A FULL REPLACE, NOT AN ADDITION.
        AreaMstResponse::readParam @0x13CBB48 opens with
            if (field != 0) goto set_field;
            if (row   != 0) goto new_row;
            AreaMstList::shared(); AreaMstList::removeAllObjects();
        so row 0 / field 0 wipes the client's entire AreaMstList before the
        rows are rebuilt.  Whatever is sent here IS the client's area table:
        every area omitted stops existing, Grand Gaia's included.  Send the
        complete table or nothing at all.  (AreaMstList::addObject @0x13096B4
        is a bare retain+push_back with no dedupe, so this clear is the only
        thing keeping a second send from doubling every area.)

        Why send it at all: the client otherwise uses the copy it downloaded
        from our own CDN (deploy/game_content/mst/Ver669_vj6fEsg7.dat, which
        decodes to the F_AREA_MST_Ver669 our deploy/mst/area_mst.json is
        byte-identical to), and that copy carries 88 Vortex areas — 10 with no
        dungeon behind them, 34 named in Japanese or mojibake, and 13 that are
        another area's tile a second time.  Sending a curated table is what
        removes those tiles; see ServerCache::clientAreaMst.

        Order matters here too, and it is this field that decides it.
        ⚠ THE VORTEX LIST IS DESCENDING — HIGHEST display_order IS THE TOP TILE.
        AreaSPSelectScene::setDungeonList builds its own
        pair<dispOrder, AreaMst*> vector and sorts it at 0x183F2D4 with
        std::__sort<std::greater<...>>.  AreaMstList::getActiveList @0x1309EDC
        sorts the same pair type with std::__less and is ASCENDING, but it is a
        DIFFERENT consumer and citing it here is what put Parade Garden at the
        bottom of the list on the first attempt.  Cite the scene, not the list.
        """
    }

    field energy_recover type="DefineEnergyRecover" {
        key "VkoZ5t3K"
        doc "The header energy-refill threshold only (DefineEnergyRecover): the player's max energy, so tapping the Energy gauge says \"max\" exactly when it is."
    }
}

// ReinforcementInfo is defined in net/reinforcement_info.kdl (xZH6EIQ7 â€” 47
// fields; the local stub here was kept-as-placeholder until the schema was
// decoded).
//
// FriendGetResp emits BOTH wire keys:
//   - xZH6EIQ7 (ReinforcementInfoResponse) â€” populates ReinforcementInfoList,
//     which a captured production response (server/deploy/log_res/2o4axPIC_*.json)
//     confirms is the canonical key the BF server returns here even when empty
//   - tojMy68W (FriendInfoResponse) â€” populates FriendInfoList; per the IDA
//     dispatch table this is the "friend list" singleton used by other UIs
//     (general friend roster, friend detail screen).  Cheap to populate both
//     since we have the same source data.
//
// The Reinforcement picker UI almost certainly reads from one of these two
// singletons; sending both removes the ambiguity until we identify which.
// FriendInfo schema is in net/friends.kdl, ReinforcementInfo in
// net/reinforcement_info.kdl.

json FriendGetResp {
    doc "FriendGet (2o4axPIC) response â€” the friend list that populates the Reinforcement / squad-select picker.  Emits both xZH6EIQ7 (ReinforcementInfoList) and tojMy68W (FriendInfoList) with the same source data."

    field reinforce_info type="[ReinforcementInfo]" {
        key "xZH6EIQ7"
        doc "Friend list entries under the reinforce/helper picker key â€” matches the captured prod response shape."
    }

    field friend_info type="[FriendInfo]" {
        key "tojMy68W"
        doc "Same entries under the friend-info-list key â€” populates FriendInfoList for any UI that reads from there."
    }

    field friend_point_info type="[FriendPointInfo]" {
        key "6e4b7sQt"
        doc "The Honor a borrowed helper is worth -- see FriendPointInfo.  Sent here as well as on UserInfo because THIS is the reply that draws the picker: every card calls ReinforcementInfo::getFriendPoint, which reads this singleton.  One full row, never an empty array."
    }
}

json FriendApplyResp {
    doc """
    FriendApply (WUNi08YL) response -- the roster as it stands AFTER the friend
    was accepted.

    THE REPLY HAS TO CARRY IT.  FriendInfoList is a client singleton filled at
    login and refreshed only when a reply sends tojMy68W; nothing re-fetches it
    when a friend request is accepted, so returning an empty body left the new
    friend on the server and missing from the Friend List until the next
    FriendGet.  Same discipline as the present box and the unit dictionary:
    every handler refreshes the cache it just changed.

    xZH6EIQ7 is deliberately NOT sent.  This reply lands while the mission
    result screens are still up and still reading ReinforcementInfoList, and
    that list is a full replace -- sending the roster alone would drop the
    strangers out from under the scene.  FriendGet rebuilds it on the way into
    the next picker anyway.
    """

    field friend_info type="[FriendInfo]" {
        key "tojMy68W"
        doc "The COMPLETE roster.  FriendInfoResponse::readParam @0x13D93C0 calls FriendInfoList::removeAllObjects on row 0 field 0, so this is a full replace and a partial send would erase the rest."
    }

    field friend_point_info type="[FriendPointInfo]" {
        key "6e4b7sQt"
        doc "Honor rates, resent so the refreshed cards keep their amounts -- the singleton's ctor defaults both to the literal 0."
    }
}

